Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> Derek Ray wrote:
>> I'm not so sure; the Wizard has a lot of late-game advantages from his
>> spells and curse absorption.
> 
> If I wasn't clear enough I want to emphasize; I think the Wizards are
> difficult in the early game, later there's not that much difference
> to most other classes in many ways.

For this, I cannot agree.  The wizard has the complete spell toolbox
available and #enhanced, and by the late game he gets to whip it all out
with hungerless casting anytime he feels like it.  The Amulet makes it
expensive (if you don't just drop it on the floor where you're standing
for a turn), but the Eye's regen more than makes up for it.

No other class gets this sort of benefit so easily.

>> Late game non-spellcasters (barb, cave) have a definite disadvantage
>> compared to a wizard if they haven't found the appropriate
>> wands/scrolls/armor (jumping boots, anyone?) to compensate for not
>> having the spells.  There's a huge difference between having to wade
>> through Astral blow-by-blow and being able to jump past most of it.
> 
> I disagree. Astral allows for many options. Teleport is number one!

Jumping combined with teleport and conflict is #1.  Wizards get the
first two as spells they can write if necessary; other classes must make
do with boots and wands.

> Barbarians can have excellent long swords at skilled.

Killing things is not an efficient means of navigating Astral.  Summon
storms from an Archon or titan, for example, will slow you down
enormously until you can wade over to the summoner and kill him... and
hopefully more summoners haven't appeared in the meantime.

The more turns you spend on Astral, the more dangerous it can become.
So your #1 goal must at all times be to minimize turns.

> Cavemen have,
> despite its one serious drawback, an excellent expert artifact. 

But that serious drawback affects approximately 1/3 of the monsters on
Astral, making it weaker than the Barbarian's "excellent longswords".
And cavemen don't get #twoweapon, making it even harder to deal damage
efficiently.

> And even a barbarian (at level 18 with a robe and not too stupid) 

...Already assuming the barbarian isn't using a cloak of magic
resistance, has found the appropriate gain ability potions to get his
spellcasting up, and isn't loaded down with metal armor...

>has very good MM spellcasting abilities; but it's _not really necessary_.

See, here you're still focused on killing things.  By Astral, a wizard
isn't just zipping around with MM, he's packing force bolt, knock,
wizard lock, jumping, and teleport away, as WELL as anything else he's
been able to pick up along the way, including "haste self" if he picked
up jump boots.  He just has better options available in ALL situations.

Even if you let the barbarian cast magic missile with some hope of the
same efficiency the wizard has, it doesn't matter; MM still isn't the
best way to deal with Astral.

As far as "not necessary", if you spend 200,000 turns overpreparing with
any of the given unlimited-resource flaws available in the game, sure,
you can make the case that Astral can be meleed the whole way with a tin
opener.  But given an equal amount of preparation, the wizard still has
an easier time of it.

>> And just how effective would you say casting force bolt is when you
>> DON'T have a permanent, infinite source of one-turn Elbereth that you
>> got from a fortunate early altar?
> 
> These two points in my statement (spell, E-word) were independent. 

Are they?  I'm not so sure.

> The force bolt actually hits very well and doesn't miss often; 

Again, I've watched a LOT of early-game wizards on NAO -- usually the
ones we see flash past every 15 minutes with "___, died to a gnome lord"
or some such very frequently.  They lead off at XL1 with some force
bolts, and when those miss, they're left standing there at 0 Pw hoping
Felis Humongous jumps in to save them.  Not well thought out on their
part.  Or, worse, they hit -- and then the werejackal comes around the
corner and summons more doggies.

> one zap hits
> also many monsters standing in a row - you just have to take advantage
> of that. 

If you can outrun monsters well enough to get them all standing in a
row, you can outrun the monsters well enough to fight them one on one as
necessary.  The monsters you're worried about at a low level are not the
ones you can outrun -- it doesn't matter how many times force bolt
misses a zombie or a mummy.

> The E-word, OTOH, made it unnecessary to flee; 

...Thus meaning that whether force bolt hit, missed, or went off in the
wrong direction, you didn't have to worry about outrunning monsters.

> the point was
> that it is very powerful *if* you find an appropriate altar. 

This way to Minetown... pick up the daggers along the way.  I'm sure
you're familiar with all the means to convert altars, especially if you
dove to Sokoban and got reflection.

> Without an
> altar and thus without Magicbane, life expectations of Wizards are much
> lower; which makes it a hard class; in the early game; or until you get
> that weapon. In my opinion.

The problem is, MANY of the non-melee classes are hard in the early
game.  But none of them are as easy as Wizard later, and a sufficiently
skilled player KNOWS how to survive with a non-melee class in the early
game.

>> Thrown daggers from the Mines and your pet with dust Elbereth, etc. 
> 
> Dust Elbereth is "incompatible" with throwing; it will blur. 

First: "These two points in my statement (daggers, E-word) were
independent."  Dust Elbereth while your pet ices the critters; it works
great.  Or just throw daggers before they get to you -- you can do a lot
of damage with thrown daggers to soften things up for your pet.

Second, as Mike has already pointed out, dust E actually does work that
well even if you're fighting, force bolting, or doing whatever on it.
Things run from you.  Let them take a step, and then zap them or throw a
dagger or four.  Do it right and you can take down even early water
demons generated from stupid-fountain-quaffing.

>> Besides,
>> trying to kill everything in the early game is one of the mistakes
>> early-game wizards make.
> 
> Pardon? Killing something like "as much as possible" keeps me alive.

Do you fully explore every level in the early game?
Do you kill everything if you do?
Do you close (and lock, if you can) doors behind you that don't block
the stairs?
Do you try to walk through the Mines, or do you trip the first dwarf you
see and dig to Minetown?

Sure, you have to kill some things no matter what.  But the number of
monsters you have to kill to achieve your objectives is not so high as
you think.  If it were, nobody could do pacifist.

